<div id="2026-11-12-schema-compare-numeric-default" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-11-12 — Schema comparison showed permanent "Update needed" on numeric defaults (0 vs 0.0000)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> <code>Admin::SchemaComparison::_fields_equivalent</code> now compares a DEFAULT numerically when the column type is numeric (<code>integer, boolean, bigint, smallint, mediumint, decimal, numeric, float, double, real</code>), instead of as a string. New helpers <code>_is_numeric_type</code> and <code>_numeric_default</code>; a non-literal default (<code>NULL</code>, <code>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code>, <code>b'0'</code>, an enum member) still falls back to string comparison, and varchar/text defaults are untouched.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> MariaDB reports a DECIMAL default padded to the column scale — <code>DEFAULT 0</code> on <code>decimal(8,4)</code> comes back as <code>0.0000</code> — while a Result file declares <code>default_value =&gt; '0'</code>. The string compare called that drift, so <code>discount_percent</code> showed "Update needed" with type, size and nullability already identical, and clicking <em>Update Table</em> could never clear it: the emitted <code>MODIFY … DEFAULT 0</code> is re-normalised to <code>0.0000</code> by MariaDB, so the next comparison reported the same drift again. 113 decimal columns in the Result files carry a numeric default, so this was a whole class of unfixable false drift, not one row.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>lib/Comserv/Controller/Admin/SchemaComparison.pm</code> (<code>_is_numeric_type</code>, <code>_numeric_default</code>, <code>_fields_equivalent</code>); <code>t/schema_compare_numeric_default.t</code> (12 assertions: padded zero equals zero, padded non-zero equals short form, quoted numeric, <code>0.01</code> vs <code>0</code> still drift, <code>NULL</code> vs <code>0</code> still drift, varchar <code>0.0000</code> vs <code>0</code> still drift, bit literal and <code>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code> rejected by the numeric reader).</p>
</div>
